 <section xml:id="Section-2">
<title>Flipping Coins</title>

      <p>Here is a common quant interview style question. Suppose you flip a coin until you get heads. How many flips do you expect this to take?</p>

      <p>Here the random variable is <m>X(s)</m> is the number of flips it takes to get heads. The probability space is infinite. Here we want essentially,</p>
      <me>\sum_{i=1}^{\infty} i P(\text{First Heads after }i\text{ flips}).</me>
      <p>You could compute this directly, or we could use linearity of expectation. You can compute this directly as an infinite series.</p>

      <exercise xml:id="exercise-first-heads-probability">
        <statement>
          <p>Explain why the probability of the first heads appearing after <m>i</m> flips is <m>1/2^{i}</m>.</p>
        </statement>
      </exercise>

      <p>Using the result of the exercise, you get the expectation</p>
      <me>\sum_{i=1}^{\infty} i (1/2)^{i},</me>
      <p>and then you could use calculus tricks to compute it. But maybe you're looking at that and don't remember what a so-called “calculus” is. Instead you think there must be some better way. Indeed there is.</p>

      <theorem xml:id="theorem-first-heads-expectation">
        <statement>
          <p>The expected number of flips until reaching a first heads is <m>2</m>.</p>
        </statement>
        <proof>
          <p>You can split <m>X</m> into two random variables. Let <m>Y</m> be a variable that is <m>1</m> is you flip heads first and <m>0</m> otherwise. Let <m>Z</m> be a random variable that is <m>0</m> if you flip heads first and the number of flips until you reached your first heads otherwise.</p>

          <p><alert>Claim 1</alert>: <m>X = Y + Z</m>. The informal way to explain this is that <m>X</m> is the amount of time you wait until your first heads. If your first flip is a heads, <m>X = 1</m>, <m>Y = 1</m>, and <m>Z = 0</m> by definition. Hence, <m>X = Y + Z</m>. Otherwise, <m>X</m> is still the number of flips until the first heads, <m>Y = 0</m>, and <m>Z</m> is the number of flips until the first heads. Thus, <m>X = Y + Z</m> in both cases. Please feel free to use this kind of informal reasoning on the exercises.</p>

          <p><alert>Claim 2:</alert> <m>\mathbb{E}[Y] = 1/2</m>. This is direct, <m>Y = 1</m> if the first flip is heads, which happens with probability <m>1/2</m>. It is <m>0</m> otherwise, so the expected value is <m>1(1/2) + 0 = 1/2</m>.</p>

          <p><alert>Claim 3:</alert> <m>\mathbb{E}[Z] = 1/2(\mathbb{E}[X]+1)</m>. Note that <m>Z</m> is only nonzero with probability <m>1/2</m>. If <m>Z</m> is nonzero, the first flip is tails, and <m>Z</m> is the amount of time until reaching heads. The first flip being tails gives no information other than a flip has already occur. Thus, the expected amount of time after making the first flip until reaching heads is <m>\mathbb{E}[X]</m>. The expected overall number of flips is <m>\mathbb{E}[X]+1</m>. Since this case only arises with probability <m>1/2</m>, we obtain</p>
          <me>\mathbb{E}[Z] = 1/2(\mathbb{E}[X]+1).</me>

          <p>Finally, then putting this together,</p>
          <md>
            <mrow>\mathbb{E}[X] &amp;= \mathbb{E}[Y + Z]</mrow>
            <mrow>&amp;= \mathbb{E}[Y] + \mathbb{E}[Z]</mrow>
            <mrow>&amp;= 1/2 + 1/2(\mathbb{E}[X] +1).</mrow>
          </md>
          <p>By subtract <m>1/2\mathbb{E}[X]</m> on both sides, this yields</p>
          <me>\mathbb{E}[X]/2 = 1/2 + 1/2 = 1,</me>
          <p>so <m>\mathbb{E}[X] = 2</m>.</p>
        </proof>
      </theorem>

      <p>While it requires some thought, it is much easier to calculate and a better proof strategy to use this approach.</p>
    </section>
